Z||000000||Pontiaddict||01-12-2001||04:31 PM||||First off, I would like to say that the 2.5 is in my sisters Grand Am but to keep it Fiero related, I can't fix my car until this gets fixed.<P>It's a 1990 Grand Am with coil pack Ignition and the newer style TBI. When you start it up it barely runs and when you shut it off you can see lots of gasoline in the intake manifold. When It;s running, you can also see the fuel spray contains more gas than should be there when it's just idling.<P>The Coolant temp sensor, 02 sensor, throttle position sensor, EGR valve, and fuel pressure regulator diaphragm have been changed recently. (the car was driven for months with most of the new parts so I know that they are good) The coolant sensor was bad but changing it didn't fix the problem. <P>Any Ideas?||||reg||9
Z||000001||88formula||01-12-2001||05:27 PM|||| Make sure the TPS is installed correctly. I think you may have a bad Map sensor. The map sensor senses engine load by reading engine vacuum. When vacuum is low at a load condition the computer sees this through the map sensor readings and gives the engine more gas. They don't always throw a code either. Make sure manifold vacuum is hooked up to the map sensor.  <P>I’m don't have the time to explain how to test one right now so you will need to get a manual and figure that part out or someone here can tell you. <P>Good luck.  <P>[This message has been edited by 88formula (edited 01-12-2001).]<p>[This message has been edited by 88formula (edited 01-12-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Pontiaddict||01-12-2001||06:44 PM||||I've got one of those cheap LED style sensor testers. I have checked all of the sensors I could find but the tester does is verify that the voltage changes but I don't know if the values are correct. The TPS value is checked when the car starts so it doesn't have or need an adjustment on it.<P>Thanks for responding, any other ideas will be appreciated.||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||01-12-2001||07:39 PM||||That much fuel flowing could be a hung injector or the injector O ring leaking. The following test works for all the DIS motors and many others.<P>The 1227748 DIS ECM only runs the fuel pump a few seconds and shuts off. (At least if the car isn't running.) This isn't long enough to really test with.<P>Find the ALDL. Get a cheap accessory cord for the lighter socket at radish rack or someplace. (Avoid the ones with fuses or replace the fuse with a 10 amp one.) Connect the positive lead of the ligher cord to ALDL terminal G. <B>MAKE SURE IT IS TERMINAL G. IF YOU CONNECT TO THE WRONG ONE YOU COULD FRY THE ECM!</B> Terminal G is the fuel pump test lead. if the injector is hung or has split an o ring then gas will flow as soon as the pump spins up. have someone watch the TBI while you connect to terminal G. unplug imediatly if fuel flows.||||reg||1
